Kandor - patch 9: hold Apple's own Navi driver off on Tahoe until OCLP's Navi patch is installed
Apply after patches 1-8 (see README.md). Author: ne1rom, with assistance from Claude (Anthropic)
SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only

On Tahoe, before OCLP's Navi root patch, Apple's own Navi driver (AMDRadeonX6000Framebuffer 7.x) attaches to a
Navi eGPU on Macs without AVX2; WindowServer then aborts in CoreDisplay_CreateDisplayForCGXDisplayDevice on every
start, so no monitor (built-in GPU included) shows anything and OCLP's root patch can't be run from its window.
While Apple's own framebuffer is loaded, the probe of every Navi controller class returns no match: the card stays on the PCI bus, OCLP detects it and offers "AMD Navi", and the
Mac starts normally on its built-in GPUs. OCLP's Monterey framebuffer is never held: it is recognised by a symbol
only it has (AmdDalHelper::setPowerAdjustment, resolved with solveSymbol like patches 5 and 8) or by the OCLP-only
patterns of patch 7; either one is enough. Tahoe (kernel 25) and later, krygpu=AMD, CPUs without AVX2 only. Off
with -krynonavihold.

Tested on Tahoe 26.7.3 / OCLP 3.0.0-rc.5 with an RX 6900 XT (Navi 21), eGPU and a monitor on the Mac's HDMI port:
- before OCLP's root patch, with it: no WindowServer restart, login window on HDMI, the eGPU on the bus without a
  controller, OCLP offered "AMD Navi"; root patch from OCLP's window, kandor.sh shim-patch, restart: status all
  green, Metal and OpenCL on the 6900;
- control with -krynonavihold: Apple's AMDRadeonX6000_AmdRadeonControllerNavi21 attaches and WindowServer aborts
  ~0.35 s after every start (SIGABRT, assert in CoreDisplay_CreateDisplayForCGXDisplayDevice); boot text only on HDMI;
- with OCLP's framebuffer installed: nothing held, the eGPU works as before.

